==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[Image:Venn3tab.svg|thumb|250px|A 3-set '''Venn diagram''', which is split by contours into 8 simply connected regions.]]

===Etymology===
Named after {{w|John Venn}} (1834–1923), British mathematician and philosopher.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[w:John Venn|Venn]] [[diagram]]}}

# {{set theory}} A diagram representing some [[set]]s by contours of closed shapes, such as circles or ellipses (and sometimes also the [[universal set]] as a rectangle enclosing all of these shapes), and indicating the relationships between the sets: by overlapping the shapes to show that the corresponding sets have a [[non-empty]] [[intersection]], and by possibly (but not necessarily) enclosing all of the sets (which are proper subsets of the universal set) within a universal set (represented typically by a rectangle); such that the total number of simply connected regions is <math style="vertical-align:-0%;">2^n</math>, where ''n'' is the number of depicted sets which are proper subsets of the universal set.

